uae_cpu_2021: a 68k to AArch64 translator

The interpreter-only core gets a translator beside it, in jit_arm64/. It is
compiled when the build defines JIT_ARM64 and used when the "jit" preference
is set; without the define nothing here changes.

The design, in four rules, is at the head of jit_arm64.cpp: the 68k registers
live in AArch64 registers for as long as translated code runs, the condition
codes are the host's NZCV, anything the translator does not know is run by
the interpreter's own handler on the same register file, and blocks branch
to each other directly.

A flush of the 68040 instruction cache raises an epoch. A block whose epoch
is older leaves for the execution loop, which compares its source byte for
byte with what memory holds before the block may run again: an exact
compare, not a checksum, and no translated instruction is rewritten for it.
flush_internals(), which every CINV and CPUSH handler already calls, is
where the core tells the translator; FlushCodeCache() is the host's side.

The host supplies executable memory and a call between two runs of
translated code (JIT_ARM64_OWN_MEMORY, JIT_ARM64_OWN_POLL); a hosted build
has defaults for both.

codegen_arm64.h is UAE4ARM's AArch64 emitter (TomB, 2019, GPL-2.0-or-later),
unchanged from rcarmo/macemu-jit at ba4a541.

Developed in kanjitalk755/macemu's tree (branch jit-aarch64 of the author's
clone), where jit-test/ holds its vectors, its fuzzer and MacBench. On an
Apple M3 MacBench runs in 3.9 s against 40.6 s interpreted.

